ZERO-PARAMETER COMPARISON: each kernel's slope law with a_0 FIXED by Lambda, no fitting anywhere
==============================================================================================================================
  10 usable bins spanning g_bar = 1.72e-12 to 7.15e-10 (y = 0.018 to 7.64 on the canonical footing)
  kernel                         footing        chi^2   dof  chi^2/dof   free parameters
  Route A (operative)            canonical       19.3    10       1.93   0 (a_0 from Lambda)
  Route A (operative)            alt             15.5    10       1.55   0 (a_0 from Lambda)
  sqrt(1+1/y) (equation book)    canonical       32.2    10       3.22   0 (a_0 from Lambda)
  sqrt(1+1/y) (equation book)    alt             26.0    10       2.60   0 (a_0 from Lambda)
  simple mu = x/(1+x)            canonical       23.2    10       2.32   0 (a_0 from Lambda)
  simple mu = x/(1+x)            alt             18.0    10       1.80   0 (a_0 from Lambda)
  deep MOND == the BTFR          canonical      136.6    10      13.66   0 (a_0 from Lambda)
  deep MOND == the BTFR          alt            136.6    10      13.66   0 (a_0 from Lambda)
  Newton, no boost               canonical     1093.8    10     109.38   0 (a_0 from Lambda)
  [PASS] A the parameter-free prediction is not refuted, and it beats the restatement: with a_0 fixed by the cosmological constant and nothing fitted, the Route A slope law fits the measured RAR slope profile better than the deep-MOND slope of 1/2 -- which is the BTFR's own prediction for this observable, and is what a restatement of v^4 = G M_b a_0 would give   (Route A chi^2 = 19.3 (canonical) / 15.5 (alt) vs deep-MOND 1/2 136.6 on 10 bins)
  [PASS] B the observable discriminates between kernels at all -- if every kernel gave the same chi^2 the measurement would be empty   (best Route A (operative) (alt) chi^2 = 15.5; worst Newton, no boost chi^2 = 1093.8)

  THE FAIR KERNEL COMPARISON -- each kernel allowed its OWN best a_0, so only the SHAPE of Phi(y) is judged:
    Route A (operative)            best a_0 = 1.538e-10   chi^2 =    13.3 on 10 bins, 1 parameter
    sqrt(1+1/y) (equation book)    best a_0 = 1.518e-10   chi^2 =    22.5 on 10 bins, 1 parameter
    simple mu = x/(1+x)            best a_0 = 1.756e-10   chi^2 =    13.5 on 10 bins, 1 parameter
  [PASS] B2 ⚠ HALF A WIN, STATED AS HALF A WIN.  With each kernel given its own free a_0 -- so that only the SHAPE of Phi(y) is judged -- the operative Route A kernel is preferred over the equation book's sqrt(1+1/y) by Delta chi^2 ~ 9, but it is a DEAD HEAT with the 'simple' mu = x/(1+x) (Delta chi^2 < 1).  So the observable separates one rival and not the other, and Route A must not be quoted as 'the kernel the data pick'.  What it does do is what prep_2026/kernel_fingerprint/fingerprint.py proposed on paper in 2026-07 and never confronted with data, and what items 90 and 91 tried through the second derivative and could not measure   (Route A: chi^2 13.3 at a_0 1.54e-10  sqrt(1+1/y): chi^2 22.5 at a_0 1.52e-10  simple mu = x/(1+x): chi^2 13.5 at a_0 1.76e-10)

==============================================================================================================================
a_0 MEASURED FROM THE SLOPE PROFILE ALONE -- distance-free and sin-i-free by construction
==============================================================================================================================
  a_0 (slope profile, Upsilon = 0.5) = 1.5380e-10   68% [1.244e-10, 1.953e-10]   (+0.104/-0.092 dex)   chi^2/dof = 1.48
     vs canonical  9.360e-11: +0.216 dex
     vs alt        1.130e-10: +0.134 dex
     LEVERS, measured not assumed:  d log a_0/d log D = +0.0000   d log a_0/d log sin i = -0.0000   d log a_0/d log Upsilon = +1.427
     the shape channel measures a_0/Upsilon^1.43, so at Upsilon = 0.5 it sits HIGH.  The Upsilon that reconciles it: 0.708 (canonical), 0.621 (alt)
     item 76 read the SAME quantity out of the NORMALISATION channel and got 0.656 (canonical) / 0.504 (alt).  The two channels are independent in their systematics and must agree.
  [PASS] C ⚠ AGAINST INTEREST AND THEN CROSS-CHECKED: at Upsilon = 0.5 the shape channel returns a_0 HIGH by +0.16 to +0.24 dex.  That is not a discrepancy but the Upsilon lever: the shape channel measures a_0/Upsilon^1.4, and the Upsilon that reconciles it must land inside the stellar-population range 0.3-0.8 AND agree with what the normalisation channel independently required (item 76: 0.656 / 0.504).  If it did not, the two channels would be inconsistent and one of them would be wrong   (a_0 = 1.538e-10 [+0.104/-0.092 dex] at Upsilon = 0.5; reconciling Upsilon 0.708 / 0.621 against item 76's 0.656 / 0.504)
  [PASS] D the levers are what the algebra says: distance and the V_obs deprojection cancel to the numerical floor because a within-galaxy log-difference cannot see a constant factor, and Upsilon survives because it moves the point into a different g_bar bin   (|d log a_0/d log D| = 0.00000, |d log a_0/d log sin i| = 0.00000, Upsilon +1.427)

