CONTROLS
==============================================================================================================================
     injected a_0 = 9.360e-11  ->  recovered 8.464e-11  (-0.044 dex)
     injected a_0 = 1.130e-10  ->  recovered 1.075e-10  (-0.022 dex)
     injected a_0 = 3.744e-10  ->  recovered 4.053e-10  (+0.034 dex)
  [PASS] M1 INJECTION: synthetic curves that obey the kernel exactly, run through the identical binning, differencing and weighting, are read back at the injected a_0 within 0.06 dex -- the finite-difference and binning biases are quantified, not assumed away (item 25's estimator failed exactly this control)   (bias at canonical -0.044 dex)
  [PASS] M2 MUTATION: a 4x wrong a_0 must be recovered as 4x wrong   (injected 4x canonical, recovered +0.034 dex from it)
     a GLOBAL shuffle of dO does NOT give zero -- it gives +0.256 against the measured +0.649 -- because both differences are predominantly NEGATIVE (both accelerations fall outward), so a shuffle keeps their signs aligned.  That is a property of the variables, not evidence.  The mutation that IS a clean null for a LAW about the g_bar dependence is to destroy that dependence and check the profile goes FLAT:
     nor is a dO shuffle a clean null for a REGRESSION SLOPE, because <dO> is not zero and each bin has its own dB distribution, so the shuffled statistic inherits a spurious bin dependence.  The null that IS clean for the claim 'the slope depends on g_bar' is to permute the BIN LABEL, leaving every (dO, dB) pair intact and only destroying which g_bar it belongs to.  The profile must then be FLAT at the global slope:
     bin-label-permuted profile: 0.754 0.619 0.614 0.627 0.693 0.647 0.661 0.657 0.662 0.662   (global slope 0.649)
  [PASS] M3 MUTATION, corrected twice: permuting the bin label -- destroying the g_bar dependence and nothing else -- must flatten the profile, because the RISE is the entire content of the law (the constant part is the BTFR restatement and is not what is being tested)   (real profile rises 0.328 -> 0.867 (4.2 sigma); bin-permuted 0.754 -> 0.662 (-1.3 sigma), flat about the global 0.649)

==============================================================================================================================
THE ALTERNATIVE, COMPUTED BESIDE
==============================================================================================================================
  Newton with no dark matter is excluded by this observable at chi^2 = 1094 on 10 bins.
  LambdaCDM with a free halo is NOT excluded and cannot be: two halo parameters per galaxy can reproduce any
  slope profile, and the published simulation work (Navarro+2017, Ludlow+2017, Keller-Wadsley 2017) shows
  hydrodynamic LambdaCDM does reproduce an RAR of roughly this shape.  What LambdaCDM does not do is PREDICT
  the curve above from a laboratory-measurable constant with nothing fitted.  That asymmetry -- one theory
  makes the prediction, the other accommodates the measurement -- is the honest statement, and it is the
  same asymmetry item 119 recorded.  It is not a discriminating test and must not be quoted as one.

  THE RESTATEMENT TEST, EXPLICITLY:  v^4 = G M_b a_0  =>  g_obs^2 = a_0 g_bar  =>  d ln g_obs/d ln g_bar = 1/2
  identically, with a_0 only in the intercept.  The derivation CLOSES, to a constant -- so the constant part
  of the measurement is a restatement and the g_bar-DEPENDENCE is not.  Measured: the slope rises from
  0.328 +- 0.077 at g_bar = 1.72e-12 to 0.867 +- 0.104 at 7.15e-10, a rise of 4.2 sigma against the BTFR's flat 1/2.

